<!--
    kuna issue `kuna-hideshadow` (P6, sub-stage merge-aggressiveness).

    Two Varnodes copied from one common ancestor always hold the same value, so
    upstream Ghidra calls them shadows of one variable.  When the copy paths are
    not nested the merge machinery cannot fold them into one chain, and the
    emitted C repeats the assignment.  ActionHideShadow (coreaction.cc:4976) is
    the upstream pass that repairs this by re-pointing the second copy at the
    first; kuna carried it as a no-op stub although the engine half
    (Merge::hideShadows, merge.rs) was fully ported.

    Both functions are gcc -O0 x86-64, each spilling its parameters to the
    frame.

    `target` (0x1000) is the shape:

      void f(char *dest, const char *dirname, const char *name)
      { const char *p = dirname;
        if (dirname[0] != '.' || dirname[1] != 0) { while (*p) *dest++ = *p++; }
        while (*name) *dest++ = *name++; *dest = 0; }

    The short-circuit makes gcc spill `dest` twice on the way into the body, and
    the second spill copies the value the first one already holds.

    `guard` (0x1094) is the same construct with the two copies on EXCLUSIVE
    branches:

      void g(char *dest, const char *dirname, const char *name)
      { const char *p = dirname;
        if (dirname[0] != '.') { dest = name; } else { p = name; dest = name; }
        while (*p) *dest++ = *p++; *dest = 0; }

    Neither copy is live at the other's definition, so upstream's Cover test
    declines and both assignments must survive with the option ON.

    Pass 1 (option hideshadow off): the repeated copy stands -> assertion #1.
    Pass 2 (option hideshadow on): it is consolidated away -> assertion #2.
    Pass 3 (guard, on): the non-dominating pair is untouched -> assertion #3.
-->
